      "matchText": "Chinese-style",
      "term": "Chinese-style",
      "definition": "The Tang Chinese administrative and architectural model that Japan imported wholesale in this era — gridded capitals, a conscript army, ranked bureaucracy, and government conducted in classical Chinese. The Heian court treated it as the template of a legitimate, centralized state."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Sakyo",
      "term": "Sakyo",
      "definition": "The \"left capital,\" the eastern half of Heian-kyo as seen by a south-facing emperor at the city's north end. It sat on better-drained ground and became the living heart of medieval Kyoto."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Ukyo",
      "term": "Ukyo",
      "definition": "The \"right capital,\" the western half of Heian-kyo. Built on marshy ground, it was largely abandoned by the late ninth century as the population drained eastward into Sakyo."
    },
