      "matchText": "Hoo",
      "term": "Hoo",
      "definition": "A retired Japanese emperor who had taken Buddhist orders (the title means \"cloistered Dharma sovereign\"). In the Insei system, such a monk-emperor could still run the state from behind the reigning emperor — a religious twist on the cloistered-government arrangement."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Genji",
      "term": "Genji",
      "definition": "The Sino-Japanese reading of the Minamoto clan name. (Confusingly, it is also the name of the fictional hero of Murasaki Shikibu's novel — but that Prince Genji is a separate, unrelated Genji.)"
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Prince Genji",
      "term": "Prince Genji",
      "definition": "The fictional protagonist of Murasaki Shikibu's *The Tale of Genji* — a courtier hero, unrelated to the real Minamoto warrior clan that shared the \"Genji\" reading."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Heike",
      "term": "Heike",
      "definition": "The Sino-Japanese reading of the Taira clan name, the great western warrior house that fought the Minamoto in the Genpei War."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Namu Amida Butsu",
      "term": "Namu Amida Butsu",
      "definition": "The Pure Land Buddhist invocation, \"Hail Amida Buddha\" — the chant (nembutsu) believed to win rebirth in Amida's paradise for anyone who calls on his name with faith."
    },
    {
      "matchText": "Inland Sea",
      "term": "Inland Sea",
      "definition": "The Seto Inland Sea, the long sheltered waterway between Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyushu. Controlling its ports and trade routes was the foundation of the Taira clan's wealth and naval power."
